<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camille Pissarro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jacob Abraham Camille Pissarro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Camille Pissarro was a Danish-French Impressionist and Neo-Impressionist painter, born in 1830 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-amalie">Charlotte Amalie</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a>. He learned from Gustave Courbet and Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot, and in 1873 helped establish a collective society of fifteen artists, becoming the figure who held the group together; he is the only artist who showed at all eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Impressionist exhibitions, from 1874 to 1886. At 54 he took up the Neo-Impressionist style alongside Georges Seurat and Paul Signac, and he acted as a father figure to Cézanne, Seurat, Gauguin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Van Gogh</a>. Éragny was his principal home until he died in Paris in 1903.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro.jpg" alt="Camille Pissarro" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pissarro-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Café Guerbois in Paris their discussions were often led by Édouard Manet, and they were joined by <b>Camille Pissarro</b>, Paul Cézanne and Armand Guillaumin.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In the arts anarchism was close to the futurists and surrealists and to the painter <b>Camille Pissarro</b>, and later to punk bands such as Crass and the Sex Pistols.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
